   "InfoDisplay_102801014_Text": "I received a message the other day.\nNothing special, just a message from the train station to the delayed passengers. It reads: \"The Future Express apologizes to you with utmost sincerity. We'll keep our service full and complete until you reach the destination.\" I finished reading and deleted it, thinking \"This must be a mistake by the station. I haven't traveled by train for a long time.\"\nBut this was just the beginning. I kept receiving strange messages about this Future Express now and then. I transferred them to the spam at first. I live in the moment. Those sorts of long travel and future seem out of my realm. I'm busy running errands around the mine, listening to frequent complaints from those researchers. Even a small miner like me gets a little bit nervous as the year draws to its close.\nBut these constant messages are killing me. Once I snatched a moment from my job to check them up and one particular piece had captured my attention (probably from a kid): \"Daddy. The steward tells me to sit tight because the train is about to leave. We'll be in the future in no time! She would cast a spell that keeps us safe until the train arrives at the future. But I don't know about the future...\" These words reminded me of my daughter, the same ever-curiosity when she was young. I haven't seen her in a while, though. I didn't want to disappoint this young sender with all the delay and wait. So I replied: I know the future. Future is where you get to eat Star Flakes and realize all of your dreams.\nSeveral days later, this message thing struck my terminal again with a delay notice: \"We're sincerely sorry for the delay. The Future Express will seek for soon departure while ensuring you a safe and happy journey with our best.\" It even ends with something like \"The Future Express will meet you in the future.\" Another text comes right in: \"When do we get to leave? Lil. You keeps asking me about this. I can only respond with empty promises, telling her that 'Soon we will be leaving for the future.' The lobby is full of anxious passengers, each clutching to an urgent desire to leave. And myself included. I received some food from a good Samaritan and that relaxed my nerves a bit. *sigh* When do we get to leave...\" This looks like a message for the train station to me but somehow it reached my terminal. Glad that I had a soft time those days. Urged by a passing thought to cheer the sender, I replied: The train will leave soon. Be patient.\nIt never really cuts off the messages. I still receive them now and then. During a meal with my colleagues, I brought up the topic and all those intricate and haywire messages in my terminal. I sometimes felt compelled to comfort those frustrated passengers.\nThey said in utter doubt, that there was no such thing as the Future Express.\nSo I showed those messages to them and met their confused stares. The elder one of my colleagues chimed in, recalling some sort of rail station around the place in the past. It was used as an escape route during a horrible disaster. People named this train \"The Future Express\", carrying a message that you could ride with it until the bitter end and arrive at the future. Ages had veiled the rest of the story when they only came along the tongues of the past generations. Even he didn't know what happened later. I can't help but think, if the Future Express truly belongs to a forgotten past, then why would I receive these messages...",
   "InfoDisplay_102801014_Title": "Strange Messages",
-  "InfoDisplay_102801015_Text": "Dim Forest Survey Team Diary: I\n\nWe conducted a comprehensive survey and inspection of the Dim Forest and have come to the decision to revive the old research site of the Forest Nature Reserve. A rearrangement and categorization of the legacy data stored inside was conducted. During this process, we even discovered seeds and specimens of some plant species that have long since been deemed extinct. These precious seeds and specimens can help us gain a better understanding of the ecological changes the Dim Forest has experienced and the impact the Lament has placed on this area. \n\nNext, we will perform a comprehensive survey of various resources of the Forest, such as soil, water, etc., and focus on studying the local TD population of Hoochiefs. Based on previous reports, there have been several assault cases involving these Hoochiefs. Explorers who have encountered these TDs claimed that they frequently appear in groups. With this information in mind, we require all investigators entering the Dark Forest to travel in teams and take precautionary measures when necessary. \n\n(Cangmin: This is information regarding the establishment of the Dim Forest’s Department of Ecology. Long after the ending of the Lament, the situation in the Forest gradually stabilized, inviting researchers to conduct studies.)\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: II\n\n10:00 A.M.\n\nSeveral Hoochiefs were found near the research institute. Through observation, it was found that these Hoochiefs possess group consciousness to a certain degree and perform physical gestures to communicate with each other. All personnel in the institute have been notified to maintain a safe distance with the Hoochiefs.\n\n1:00 P.M.\n\nAppearance of a second Hoochief.\n\n2:45 P.M.\n\nAppearance of a second Hoochief group. A small-scale conflict occurred between the two groups. One of the groups seemed to be covered in some kind of spores. The Hoochiefs without spores were defeated and fled the scene.\n\n6:00 P.M.\n\nThe Hoochiefs attacked the research institute and one was captured. The institute will shelter the Hoochief.\n\nThe behavioral characteristics of these Hoochiefs will be recorded and the engineering team are requested to install bionic cameras for our research. In the mean time, the Waveband of the Hoochief will be thoroughly analyzed.\n\n(Cangmin: Study currently at preliminary stage)\n\n(Qiaomu: The spores were later confirmed to be poisonous.)\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: III\n\nWe deployed several bionic cameras to observe the Hoochief groups. The following cameras were deployed. Three in the form of bushes, two in boulders, and one in Hoochief form. The Hoochief camera was able to imitate their movements to a certain extent, but were relatively robotic and failed to perform complex actions. In this round of filming, two bush cameras were destroyed, with the boulder cameras being used by the Hoochiefs as throwing weapons, resulting in critical damage to their lenses.\n\nThe Hoochief camera continued to follow the Hoochiefs and filmed their leader. The Hoochiefs did not show any aggression towards it, but the camera was immobilized due to a malfunction in its motion systems. The Hoochiefs later performed a \"burial\" for it around the banyan tree.\n\nWe will sweep the area around the banyan tree and recover the cameras.\n\n(Qiaomu: The bionic cameras really came in clutch. Real MVPs!)\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: IV\n\nThe sheltered Hoochief is covered in spores emitting a red glow. Researchers who came into close contact with the Hoochief for an extended period of time exhibited signs of coughing and difficulty breathing. As a result, researchers have been restricted from making close contact. \n\nDays later, the Hoochief began to show signs of crazed tendencies. It continues to devastate its surroundings while scratching its own body and coughing constantly. \n\nFurther observation of the Hoochief is required. \n\nAdditionally, we have observed an increase in the appearances of Hoochiefs near the research institute, likely influenced by the sheltered Hoochief's frequency. This requires special attention. \n\nAn investigation into the Hoochief leader is to be carried out as soon as possible.\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: V\n\nFeilian Beringal, Howler, Overlord Class Tacet Discord. \n\nBased on our studies, the Feilian Beringal resides around the Giant Banyan and is the leader of the Hoochief group. The creature maintains highly aggressive territorial behavior and will show strong hostility towards intruders. Several of our survey teams received major setbacks during their investigation of the Beringal.\n\nThe Feilian Beringal, along with the Hoochiefs under its command, are mimetic creatures capable of imitating the behavior of the apes that once existed in the Dim Forest, bearing a certain level of intelligence. they copy humans to wield simplistic tools and weapons, and will also comfort and protect their companions.\n\nIn the meantime, further spread of spore infection was observed. Hoochiefs covered in spores tend to be more aggressive. Caution is advised.\n\nResearch studies of this phenomenon are currently underway.\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: VI\n\nWith the assistance of the Midnight Rangers, we carried out a special operation on the Feilian Beringal. Through great efforts, we managed to put a tracking bracelet on the Beringal, which will help us monitor its movements in the forest and avoid any direct confrontations.\n\nDuring this operation, we discovered that the number of Hoochiefs infected with the spores was much higher than we had initially estimated, and the rate of the spore's spread was faster than the prediction models had shown. The spores can significantly affect the qualities of surrounding soil, water resources, and other environmental substances, and cause near-irreversible damage to the human body. Given that the Hoochiefs are the source of transmission of the spores, their expulsion from the area will be put in place. However, some researchers believe that the Hoochiefs are not the source of the infection itself, and have submitted a secondary proposal to begin research on spore antibodies.\n\n(Senming: My mentor was the one who initiated the antibody research. She placed hopes to resolve the Forest's complications through this way.)\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: VII\n\nProgress on the research of antibodies has been considerable. We believe a result is soon within reach.\n\nHaste expulsion of the Hoochiefs failed to yield meaningful effects, and in turn, triggered more frequent Hoochief attacks. To minimize and avoid escalation of this situation, we've changed our approach and instead focused on sheltering the apes by helping to remove the spores from their bodies and alleviate their emotions.\n\nWe hope that this will help us to better carry out our work.\n\n\n\nDim Forest Survey Team Diary: VIII\n\nA series of complications, in forms such as Hoochief disturbances, led to the loss of the majority of our research data.\n\nHowever, while scavenging for data, we were still able to witness the valiant efforts made by our predecessors on the Dim Forest.\n\n<ano=Ecoacoustics>Ecological</ano> research is a complicated study. Topics such as finding critical entry points and how to begin our research are all topics we need to be mindful of.\n\nFortunately, we now have more time to ponder.",
